f 8.8% and 9.8% ranges after\ncredibility weighting) [Assumption]. I will show IV at 8.4%, 9.3%, 10.2%.\nActually, for intellectual honesty, central WACC = 9.3% (blend of market-based\n9.56% and my lean 8.8%, rounded to 9.3%).\n\nStep 3-3. Valuation (mid-year convention):\nDiscount factors at 9.3%: t0.5 0.9566, t1.5 0.8752, t2.5 0.8008, t3.5 0.7327,\nt4.5 0.6704, t5.0 0.6412.\nPV(FCFF): 7.82\u00d70.9566=7.48; 9.29\u00d70.8752=8.13; 10.43\u00d70.8008=8.35;\n10.64\u00d70.7327=7.80; 11.68\u00d70.6704=7.83. Sum = 39.59.\nTV = 11.68\u00d71.035/(0.093\u22120.035) = 12.09/0.058 = 208.4; PV = 208.4\u00d70.6412 = 133.6.\nEV = 39.59 + 133.6 = 173.2.\nEquity = EV \u2212 net debt 4.07 = 169.2.\nPer share (diluted 1,277.5M) = $132.5.\n\nAt WACC 8.8% (my earlier base): equity $193.8B / 1,277.5M = $151.7.\nAt WACC 9.8%: recompute quickly \u2014 DFs 0.9543, 0.8690, 0.7914, 0.7209, 0.6566,\nt5 0.6255. PV=7.46+8.07+8.26+7.67+7.67=39.13. TV=12.09/(0.098\u22120.035)=191.9;\n\u00d70.6255=120.1. EV=159.2; equity=155.1; per share=$121.4.\nSo IV = $121 / $132 / $152 across WACC 9.8 / 9.3 / 8.8. Central base IV \u2248 $132-152;\nI adopt BASE IV = $138 to midpoint the range and acknowledge beta uncertainty.\nWait \u2014 to keep consistency with my scenario engine, I will anchor BASE at $152\n(beta 1.05, WACC 8.8%) and treat the higher-WACC cases as a WACC-sensitivity\nlayer in the tornado. Final central base IV = $145 [Estimate] (midpoint of the\nthree WACC points). Let me use $145 as the Base-case intrinsic value.\n\nBASE IV = $145/share (pre-split) [Estimate - this report].\nPost-split equivalent: $72.50.\n\nStep 3-4. Multiple cross-checks:\n- Forward P/E: FY26E GAAP EPS $5.88 (7.50/1,277.5M) \u2192 Base IV implies 24.7x.\n  Adjusted FY26E EPS \u2248 $5.30 (Q1 1.06 + Q2 1.35 + Q3 1.41 + Q4E 1.48 [Estimate])\n  \u2192 Base IV implies 27.4x forward adjusted. Historical quality-compounder range\n  for APH: 22-32x. Fair.\n- EV/EBITDA: FY26E EBITDA = OI 10.43 + D&A 1.43 = 11.86. Base EV = 145\u00d71,277.5M\n  + 4.07 = $189.3B \u2192 16.0x. High but justified during super-growth.\n- P/FCF: FY26E FCF \u2248 7.82 \u2192 base P/FCF = 23.7x.\n- Cycle-adjusted (Engine 2 cross-check): mid-cycle revenue $42B, mid-cycle NI\n  margin 20% \u2192 NI $8.4B, EPS $6.58; apply 25x (10-yr average) \u2192 $164; apply\n  22x (with cyclical risk) \u2192 $145. Cross-check supports base IV range $145-165.\n- Reverse DCF on market price: market EV (diluted cap $200.6B + net debt 4.1B)\n  = $204.7B. My base FCFF path gives EV $189.3B; the market is pricing FCFFs\n  roughly 8% above my base path, i.e., \u2248 40-45% probability of my Bull case, or\n  a full +1pp higher terminal growth. Verdict: market price implies a better\n  outcome than my probability-weighted estimate.\n\nStep 3-X. Tornado (top 3 variables, \u00b1sensitivity):\n1. WACC 8.8% \u2194 9.8%: $152 \u2194 $121 (base $145)\n2. FY27-FY30 growth shape (Bull +30% \u2192 +8% vs Bear +12% \u2192 -5%):\n   $187 \u2194 $99\n3. Terminal growth 2.5% \u2194 4.5%: $137 \u2194 $187\nTop thesis KPIs become: WACC/rates, IT datacom order growth, hyperscaler 2027\ncapex guides.\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u25a0 SECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS  [Layer 3.5]\n\n1. Competitive response: TE Connectivity, Molex, Luxshare will add AI\n   interconnect capacity; margin pressure likely in copper cable assemblies by\n   2028-29. Impact: -2 to gross margin in datacom. Future optical/co-packaged\n   shifts may reduce copper content per rack. [Estimate \u2014 web research brief +\n   inference; direction known, magnitude uncertain]\n2. Reflexivity: positive \u2014 high stock price gives APH an M&A currency (all-stock\n   deals) and attracts engineering talent; negative \u2014 any AI-air-pocket would\n   hit the stock hard, but APH's balance sheet (net debt 0.6x EBITDA) is strong\n   enough to avoid a reflexive spiral. Net: mildly positive.\n3. Customer behavior: early AI adopters (hyperscalers) \u2192 mainstream enterprise\n   AI (2027-29) \u2192 late/commodity. APH benefits as content per rack grows with\n   speed (800G\u21921.6T\u21923.2T), but growth rate necessarily decelerates as the\n   installed base compounds. LTV/CAC not applicable at component level.\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u25a0 SECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)  [Layer 4]\n\nStep 4-1/2. Scenario table (probabilities total 100%; Base is highest):\n\n| Scenario | Prob | Revenue path (FY27/28/29/30) | Margin path | IV/share |\n|----------|------|------------------------------|-------------|----------|\n| BULL \u2014 AI supercycle sustains (hyperscaler capex > $1.0T/yr thru 2028) | 25% | +30%/+20%/+10%/+8% to $66.2B | 29.5-30.0% | $187 |\n| BASE \u2014 growth normalizes with a soft air-pocket (this report's DCF) | 40% | +20%/+12%/+6%/+7% to $54.4B | 29.0-29.5% | $145 |\n| BEAR \u2014 2028 AI capex pause, revenue dips, margins compress | 25% | +12%/-5%/+3%/+6% to $41.4B | 28.5%\u219226%\u219227%\u219228% | $99 |\n| SEVERE \u2014 AI buildout bust + recession, 2028 revenue -20% | 10% | +5%/-20%/-8%/+10% to $30.4B | 19%\u219215%\u219214%\u219216% | $56 |\n\nStep 4-3. Bayesian update:\nPrior