al drawdown base rate (40%+ drop within 3 years of a cyclical peak):\n  ~35% [Reference base rate]. Bear case (20%) explicitly includes this.\n- Shock check: my 5-year sustained-growth probability (~55-60%) vs base rate\n  (~35%) is at 1.6x \u2014 allowed (3+ justifications above), but NOT >2x, so no red flag.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\nStep 2-1. Business understanding (3 sentences):\n1) APH designs and manufactures connectors, cable, antennas, sensors, and\nhigh-speed interconnect systems used in virtually every electronic device.\n2) It sells to seven end-markets \u2014 IT datacom, comm networks, mobile devices,\nautomotive, defense, commercial aerospace, industrial \u2014 across ~40 countries.\n3) Growth comes from a decentralized engineering culture plus a 20+ year\nbolt-on acquisition program (CommScope being the largest, with FY26E sales $4.6B).\nUnderstandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat (0-3 each) + direction:\n- Intangible assets: 2/3 \u2014 engineering reputation, patents (Stable)\n- Switching costs: 3/3 \u2014 qualification cycles measured in years in defense/aero/auto\n  (Widening \u2014 design wins in AI platforms)\n- Network effects: 0/3 \u2014 none\n- Cost advantage: 2/3 \u2014 global scale, but not the low-cost leader vs Luxshare\n  (Stable)\n- Economies of scale: 2/3 \u2014 broad portfolio, 4.5% R&D leverage (Stable)\n- Regulatory barriers: 2/3 \u2014 defense/aero certifications (Stable)\n- Data/design-in moat: 1/3 \u2014 application engineering knowledge (Widening)\nTOTAL: 12/21 \u2192 WIDE MOAT. Direction: STABLE-to-WIDENING.\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch classification: FAST GROWER with Stalwart diversification.\nCheckpoint \u2014 can growth persist? Supported by content-per-box increases and M&A;\nopposed by AI-capex lumpiness. Result: yes, but at a fading rate.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial health:\n- Revenue CAGR 3-yr: 22.3% [Arithmetic \u2014 pack]; 5-yr: 21.9% [Pack \u00a77]\n- Op margin: FY25 25.4%; 10-yr median 20.4% [Pack \u00a77 \u2014 Arithmetic]\n- Net income FY25: $4.305B [Actual], +76% YoY\n- ROE: 38.1% [Aggregator \u2014 pack]; ROIC ~24% [Estimate \u2014 my calc, see 2.5]\n- FCF margin FY25: 19.0% [Arithmetic]; FCF/NI: 101.7% [Arithmetic]\n- Net debt (my definition: LT noncurrent 14.565 + current portion 0.937 + op\n  leases 0.566 \u2212 cash 11.131 \u2212 ST investments 0.304) = $4.633B [Arithmetic on\n  pack \u00a76B]. Company-stated net leverage 1.3x \u2192 net debt ~$8.8B [Estimate].\n  I use $8.8B as the conservative number.\n- Interest coverage: >15x [Estimate \u2014 FY25 interest line missing in pack]\nJudgment: EXCELLENT.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital allocation (0-15):\n1. Buyback efficiency: 2/3 (Q2'26 repurchases 1.5M @ ~$141 \u2014 reasonable, not peak\n   aggressive)\n2. M&A track record: 3/3 (CommScope accretion raised $0.15 \u2192 $0.30; decades of\n   ROIC > WACC)\n3. Capex efficiency: 2/3 (capex/revenue 4.3%; incremental ROIC >20% implied)\n4. Rationality of mix: 3/3 (dividends + buybacks + M&A + leverage 1.3x)\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3/3 (Q1 and Q2 2026 both exceeded high end of guidance)\nSubtotal: 13/15\nInsider adjustments: CEO holds <5% (no +1); CEO exercised options and sold\n~$76M (Feb 2026), $18.7M (May 2026), plus $101.9M option exercise disclosed\nAug 2026 [Estimate \u2014 Benzinga/Investing.com/MarketBeat, retrieved] \u2192 \u22122.\nFINAL: 11/15.\n\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\n- R&D: not separately disclosed in pack [Unconfirmed]. Not material for a\n  component manufacturer (R&D embedded in SG&A; no R&D capitalization adjustment\n  needed).\n- SBC: FY25 $0.135B [Actual]. Adjusted FCF = 4.378 \u2212 0.135 = $4.243B\n  [Arithmetic]. This reflects the real cash expense.\n- Operating leases: FY25 $0.566B [Actual]. Adjusted net debt (my definition +\n  leases) = $5.199B [Arithmetic]; coverage still >15x.\n- Brand/data assets: B2B \u2014 no separate brand value recognized. Data/design-in\n  already capitalised through customer qualification; no premium added.\n- Adjusted